Start arm64 User-mode Linux programs with zeroed FP and vector state

User-mode Linux gives every new program the floating-point registers of the
stub process it sampled at boot. On arm64 the stub's vector registers hold
values from its own startup code, and those depend on where the host placed
the stub, so a new guest program could see host address bits. arm64 Linux
starts every program with zeroed vector registers and zero FPSR and FPCR, and
the sampled state is now zeroed the same way.

This patch is Linux-kernel code (GPL-2.0), carried as a kernel diff under
consonance/harmony-linux/linux/patches/ per the repository's kernel-patch
exception.

---
--- a/arch/um/os-Linux/start_up.c
+++ b/arch/um/os-Linux/start_up.c
@@ -301,6 +301,10 @@ static bool __init init_seccomp(void)
 		exec_fp_regs = malloc(host_fp_size);
 		memcpy(exec_regs, regs->gp, sizeof(exec_regs));
 		memcpy(exec_fp_regs, regs->fp, host_fp_size);
+#ifdef __aarch64__
+		/* arm64 Linux starts every program with zeroed FP and vector state */
+		memset(exec_fp_regs, 0, host_fp_size);
+#endif
 
 		munmap(seccomp_test_stub_data, sizeof(*seccomp_test_stub_data));
 
